{
  "id": "80654",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Nuclear Binding Energy",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "As a simple example of the energy associated with the strong nuclear force, consider the helium atom composed of two protons, two neutrons, and two electrons. The total mass of these six subatomic particles may be calculated as:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5tubxzel604fg1u",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "bh3ha0lpo5s2jwu",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1ps5p5qn3yyuk7o",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\underset{\\mathrm{protons}}{(2\\times1.0073\\;\\mathrm{amu})}+\\underset{\\mathrm{neutrons}}{(2\\times1.0087\\;\\mathrm{amu})}+\\underset{\\mathrm{electrons}}{(2\\times0.00055\\;\\mathrm{amu})}=4.0331\\;\\mathrm{amu}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "However, mass spectrometric measurements reveal that the mass of a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " atom is 4.0026 amu, less than the combined masses of its six constituent subatomic particles. This difference between the calculated and experimentally measured masses is known as the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "mass defect"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The difference between the actual mass of an atomic nucleus and the sum of the masses of its individual protons and neutrons; occurs due to the conversion of mass into binding energy."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1618866625",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2664392855",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " of the atom. In the case of helium, the mass defect indicates a “loss” in mass of 4.0331 amu − 4.0026 amu = 0.0305 amu. The loss in mass accompanying the formation of an atom from protons, neutrons, and electrons is due to the conversion of that mass into energy that is evolved as the atom forms. The "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "nuclear binding energy"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The energy required to disassemble a nucleus into its separate protons and neutrons (or the energy released when the nucleus is formed from its constituent nucleons)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4153577012",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2774490653",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is the energy produced when the atom's nucleons are bound together; this is also the energy needed to break a nucleus into its constituent protons and neutrons. In comparison to chemical bond energies, nuclear binding energies are vastly greater. Consequently, the energy changes associated with nuclear reactions are vastly greater than those for chemical reactions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1kuo3zixxti47gh",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The conversion between mass and energy is most identifiably represented by the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "mass–energy equivalence equation"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Equation demonstrating that mass and energy are interchangeable: "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "E"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " = "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "mc"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "²."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3401407033",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "415595474",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", as stated by Albert Einstein."
              }
            ],
            "id": "bqe0iv1l38uobnj",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "b8yst1w5srbwm2r",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "mzbpjan28jobcap",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/A3/A31F9808B584CB71B1FEA56DD0BCDE4E/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "0zdyhjeomk7j73t",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "y22t5155h20dpy6",
            "items": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3207443322",
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " ",
                        "type": "text"
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                    "src": "E\\;=\\;mc^2",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "where "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "E"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is energy, "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "m"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is the mass of the matter being converted, and "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "c"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is the speed of light in a vacuum."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1143796082",
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1767791645",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "dd"
              }
            ],
            "title": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Mass–Energy Equivalence Equation "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2552189124",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "type": "dl"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "hwcrkvbn5rhb3vg",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "8c0fmd7ya83yheh",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/A3/A31F9808B584CB71B1FEA56DD0BCDE4E/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "This equation can be used to find the amount of energy that results when matter is converted into energy. Using the mass–energy equivalence equation, the nuclear binding energy of a nucleus may be calculated from its mass defect, as demonstrated in the example below. A variety of units are commonly used for nuclear binding energies, including electron volts (eV), with 1 eV equaling the amount of energy necessary to move the charge of an electron across an electric potential difference of 1 volt, making 1 eV = 1.602 × 10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−19"
              },
              {
                "text": " J."
              }
            ],
            "id": "jz5d7ld8msp92b0",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "2451124492",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "Calculation of Nuclear Binding Energy"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "lu9t41udt385mks",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Determine the binding energy for the nuclide "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " in:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ke115ub810mcdew",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " joules per mole of nuclei"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "8poq85b4iz5c2an",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " joules per nucleus"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "8k3q9fzz577x0gd",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(c)"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " MeV per nucleus"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "kl3gj8s31vqlb3a",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The mass defect for a "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " nucleus is 0.0305 amu, as shown previously. Determine the binding energy in joules per nuclide using the mass–energy equivalence equation. To accommodate the requested energy units, the mass defect must be expressed in kilograms (recall that 1 J = 1 kg m"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "/s"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ")."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "k6i38pcoog63zrd",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " First, express the mass defect in g/mol. This is quickly done considering the "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "numerical equivalence"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " of atomic mass (amu) and molar mass (g/mol) that results from the definitions of the amu and mole units. The mass defect is, therefore, 0.0305 g/mol. To accommodate the units of the other terms in the mass–energy equation, the mass must be expressed in kilograms since 1 J = 1 kg m"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "/s"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". Converting grams into kilograms yields a mass defect of 3.05 × 10"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−5"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " kg/mol. Substituting this quantity into the mass–energy equivalence equation yields:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "0jm1rryep8e7g9p",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "ezwtqr01ciftota",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "rcy6pjzgjwuw8tf",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "E\\;=\\;mc^2\\;=\\;\\frac{3.05\\;\\times\\;10^{-5}\\;\\mathrm{kg}}{\\mathrm{mol}}\\;\\times\\;\\left(\\frac{2.998\\;\\times\\;10^8\\;\\mathrm m}{\\mathrm s}\\right)^2\\;=\\;2.74\\;\\times\\;10^{12}\\;\\mathrm{kg}\\;\\mathrm m^2\\mathrm s^{-2}\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\\\\=\\;2.74\\;\\times\\;10^{12}\\;\\mathrm J\\;\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\\;=\\;2.74\\;\\mathrm{TJ}\\;\\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \\\\",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Note that this tremendous amount of energy is associated with converting a very small amount of matter (about 30 mg, roughly the mass of a typical drop of water). "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "j5vil7rnq9xf9qz",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " The binding energy for a single nucleus is computed from the molar binding energy using Avogadro’s number:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "5079h2a8gu77lmg",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "gxfsr7xefo13kxa",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "7j0f8xqmpfi11rg",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "E\\;=\\;2.74\\;\\times\\;10^{12}\\;\\mathrm J\\;\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\\;\\times\\;\\frac{1\\;\\mathrm{mol}}{6.022\\;\\times\\;10^{23}\\;\\mathrm{nuclei}}\\;=\\;4.55\\;\\times\\;10^{-12}\\;\\mathrm J\\;=\\;4.55\\;\\mathrm{pJ}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(c)"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " Recall that 1 eV = 1.602 × 10"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−19"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " J. Using the binding energy computed in part (b):"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "6jsmjqud4jfyrno",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "45ql14oij6taetb",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "enzztfs5n2y8s5f",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "E\\;=\\;4.55\\;\\times\\;10^{-12}\\;\\mathrm J\\;\\times\\;\\frac{1\\;\\mathrm{eV}}{1.602\\;\\times\\;10^{-19}\\;\\mathrm J}\\;=\\;2.84\\;\\times\\;10^7\\;\\mathrm{eV}\\;=\\;28.4\\;\\mathrm{MeV}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2963510570",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1514749839",
            "selection": {
              "anchor": {
                "offset": 0,
                "path": [
                  0,
