            },
            "type": "content"
          }
        ],
        "id": "5442964",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "example",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Because the energy changes for breaking and forming bonds are so small compared to those for breaking or forming nuclei, the changes in mass during all ordinary chemical reactions are virtually undetectable. The most energetic chemical reactions exhibit enthalpies on the order of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "thousands"
              },
              {
                "text": " of kJ/mol, equivalent to mass differences in the nanogram range (10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−9"
              },
              {
                "text": " g). On the other hand, nuclear binding energies are typically on the order of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "billions"
              },
              {
                "text": " of kJ/mol, corresponding to mass differences in the milligram range (10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−3"
              },
              {
                "text": " g)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "ylk2zj09wlne31m",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "951196641",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
